1
00:00:01,379 --> 00:00:07,359
Imagine you happen across a short movie script that describes a scene between a person and their AI assistant.

2
00:00:07,359 --> 00:00:09,999
The script has what the person asks the AI

3
00:00:09,999 --> 00:00:13,059
but the AI's response has been torn off.

4
00:00:13,059 --> 00:00:21,000
Suppose you also have this powerful magical machine that can take any text and provide a sensible prediction of what word comes next.

5
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:24,659
You could then finish the script by feeding in what you have to the machine

6
00:00:24,659 --> 00:00:27,979
seeing what it would predict to start the AI's answer

7
00:00:27,979 --> 00:00:32,980
and then repeating this over and over with a growing script completing the dialogue.

8
00:00:32,980 --> 00:00:36,640
When you interact with a chatbot, this is exactly what's happening.

9
00:00:36,640 --> 00:00:44,079
A large language model is a sophisticated mathematical function that predicts what word comes next for any piece of text.

10
00:00:44,079 --> 00:00:46,839
Instead of predicting one word with certainty, though

11
00:00:46,839 --> 00:00:51,020
what it does is assign a probability to all possible next words.

12
00:00:51,020 --> 00:00:58,899
To build a chatbot, what you do is lay out some text that describes an interaction between a user and a hypothetical AI assistant.

13
00:00:58,899 --> 00:01:03,439
You add on whatever the user types in as the first part of that interaction

14
00:01:03,439 --> 00:01:10,779
and then you have the model repeatedly predict the next word that such a hypothetical AI assistant would say in response

15
00:01:10,779 --> 00:01:12,519
and that's what's presented to the user.

16
00:01:12,519 --> 00:01:19,579
In doing this, the output tends to look a lot more natural if you allow it to select less likely words along the way at random.

17
00:01:19,579 --> 00:01:23,459
So what this means is even though the model itself is deterministic

18
00:01:23,459 --> 00:01:27,119
a given prompt typically gives a different answer each time it's run.

19
00:01:27,119 --> 00:01:32,599
Models learn how to make these predictions by processing an enormous amount of text

20
00:01:32,599 --> 00:01:34,219
typically pulled from the internet.

21
00:01:34,219 --> 00:01:38,639
For a standard human to read the amount of text that was used to train GPT-3

22
00:01:38,639 --> 00:01:41,779
for example, if they read nonstop 24-7

23
00:01:41,779 --> 00:01:44,439
it would take over 2,600 years.

24
00:01:44,439 --> 00:01:47,419
Larger models since then train on much, much more.

25
00:01:47,419 --> 00:01:51,939
You can think of training a little bit like tuning the dials on a big machine.

26
00:01:51,939 --> 00:01:57,859
The way that a language model behaves is entirely determined by these many different continuous values

27
00:01:57,859 --> 00:02:00,459
usually called parameters or weights.

28
00:02:00,459 --> 00:02:07,299
Changing those parameters will change the probabilities that the model gives for the next word on a given input.

29
00:02:07,299 --> 00:02:14,059
What puts the large in large language model is how they can have hundreds of billions of these parameters.

30
00:02:14,059 --> 00:02:18,179
No human ever deliberately sets those parameters.

31
00:02:18,179 --> 00:02:22,179
Instead, they begin at random, meaning the model just outputs gibberish

32
00:02:22,179 --> 00:02:26,619
but they're repeatedly refined based on many example pieces of text.

33
00:02:26,619 --> 00:02:30,199
One of these training examples could be just a handful of words

34
00:02:30,199 --> 00:02:37,779
or it could be thousands, but in either case the way this works is to pass in all but the last word from that example into the model

35
00:02:37,779 --> 00:02:42,439
and compare the prediction that it makes with the true last word from the example.

36
00:02:42,439 --> 00:02:52,719
An algorithm called backpropagation is used to tweak all of the parameters in such a way that it makes the model a little more likely to choose the true last word

37
00:02:52,719 --> 00:02:55,159
and a little less likely to choose all the others.

38
00:02:55,159 --> 00:02:59,579
When you do this for many, many trillions of examples

39
00:02:59,579 --> 00:03:03,479
not only does the model start to give more accurate predictions on the training data

40
00:03:03,479 --> 00:03:08,359
but it also starts to make more reasonable predictions on text that it's never seen before.

41
00:03:08,359 --> 00:03:13,519
Given the huge number of parameters and the enormous amount of training data

42
00:03:13,519 --> 00:03:18,919
the scale of computation involved in training a large language model is mind-boggling.

43
00:03:18,919 --> 00:03:25,499
To illustrate, imagine that you could perform one billion additions and multiplications every single second.

44
00:03:25,499 --> 00:03:32,679
How long do you think that it would take for you to do all of the operations involved in training the largest language models?

45
00:03:32,679 --> 00:03:35,159
Do you think it would take a year?

46
00:03:35,159 --> 00:03:38,099
Maybe something like 10,000 years?

47
00:03:38,099 --> 00:03:40,799
The answer is actually much more than that.

48
00:03:40,799 --> 00:03:44,079
It's well over 100 million years.

49
00:03:44,079 --> 00:03:47,319
This is only part of the story, though.

50
00:03:47,319 --> 00:03:49,399
This whole process is called pre-training.

51
00:03:49,399 --> 00:03:56,379
The goal of auto-completing a random passage of text from the internet is very different from the goal of being a good AI assistant.

52
00:03:56,379 --> 00:04:00,059
To address this, chatbots undergo another type of training

53
00:04:00,059 --> 00:04:03,819
just as important, called reinforcement learning with human feedback.

54
00:04:03,819 --> 00:04:10,779
Workers flag unhelpful or problematic predictions, and their corrections further change the model's parameters

55
00:04:10,779 --> 00:04:14,879
making them more likely to give predictions that users prefer.

56
00:04:14,879 --> 00:04:24,519
Looking back at the pre-training though, this staggering amount of computation is only made possible by using special computer chips that are optimized for running many

57
00:04:24,519 --> 00:04:27,619
many operations in parallel, known as GPUs.

58
00:04:27,619 --> 00:04:31,639
However, not all language models can be easily parallelized.

59
00:04:31,639 --> 00:04:36,919
Prior to 2017, most language models would process text one word at a time

60
00:04:36,919 --> 00:04:42,679
but then a team of researchers at Google introduced a new model known as the transformer.

61
00:04:42,679 --> 00:04:46,339
Transformers don't read text from the start to the finish.

62
00:04:46,339 --> 00:04:49,299
They soak it all in at once in parallel.

63
00:04:49,299 --> 00:04:54,279
The very first step inside a transformer, and most other language models for that matter

64
00:04:54,279 --> 00:04:57,579
is to associate each word with a long list of numbers.

65
00:04:57,579 --> 00:05:02,299
The reason for this is that the training process only works with continuous values

66
00:05:02,299 --> 00:05:05,559
so you have to somehow encode language using numbers

67
00:05:05,559 --> 00:05:10,359
and each of these lists of numbers may somehow encode the meaning of the corresponding word.

68
00:05:10,359 --> 00:05:16,139
What makes transformers unique is their reliance on a special operation known as attention.

69
00:05:16,139 --> 00:05:25,219
This operation gives all of these lists of numbers a chance to talk to one another and refine the meanings that they encode based on the context around

70
00:05:25,219 --> 00:05:26,719
all done in parallel.

71
00:05:26,719 --> 00:05:36,279
For example, the numbers encoding the word bank might be changed based on the context surrounding it to somehow encode the more specific notion of a riverbank.

72
00:05:36,279 --> 00:05:43,219
Transformers typically also include a second type of operation known as a feedforward neural network

73
00:05:43,219 --> 00:05:48,559
and this gives the model extra capacity to store more patterns about language learned during training.

74
00:05:48,559 --> 00:05:54,819
All of this data repeatedly flows through many different iterations of these two fundamental operations

75
00:05:54,819 --> 00:06:06,159
and as it does so, the hope is that each list of numbers is enriched to encode whatever information might be needed to make an accurate prediction of what word follows in the passage.

76
00:06:06,159 --> 00:06:11,659
At the end, one final function is performed on the last vector in this sequence

77
00:06:11,659 --> 00:06:16,639
which now has had a chance to be influenced by all the other context from the input text

78
00:06:16,639 --> 00:06:19,059
as well as everything the model learned during training

79
00:06:19,059 --> 00:06:22,179
to produce a prediction of the next word.

80
00:06:22,179 --> 00:06:27,439
Again, the model's prediction looks like a probability for every possible next word.

81
00:06:27,439 --> 00:06:32,359
Although researchers design the framework for how each of these steps work

82
00:06:32,359 --> 00:06:41,959
it's important to understand that the specific behavior is an emergent phenomenon based on how those hundreds of billions of parameters are tuned during training.

83
00:06:41,959 --> 00:06:48,019
This makes it incredibly challenging to determine why the model makes the exact predictions that it does.

84
00:06:48,019 --> 00:06:53,579
What you can see is that when you use large language model predictions to autocomplete a prompt

85
00:06:53,579 --> 00:06:56,739
the words that it generates are uncannily fluent

86
00:06:56,739 --> 00:06:59,339
fascinating, and even useful.

87
00:06:59,339 --> 00:07:10,119
If you're a new viewer and you're curious about more details on how transformers and attention work

88
